\title{Baseline & Lineage}

\p{
  The surrogate and trust-region loop follow Bafna, Jadhav, & Sweet [[BJS25]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12818) ([Taking the GP Out of the Loop](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12818)) and [yubo-research/enn](https://github.com/yubo-research/enn), replacing Gaussian Process matrix inversion with inverse-variance nearest-neighbor smoothing (Aitken [[Ait35]](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0370164600014346), Cochran [[Coc54]](https://doi.org/10.2307/3001666)). \strong{baux} extends this surrogate across non-Euclidean domain charts #{\mathcal{M}} and multi-region portfolios.
}

\p{
  Exact Gaussian Process inference over #{N} observations inverts an #{N \times N} Gram matrix in #{\mathcal{O}(N^3)} time. Bafna et al. [[BJS25]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.12818) instead treat each observation as a local location–variance estimate and combine the #{K} nearest neighbors of a query in #{\mathcal{O}(N D + N \log N)} exact flat-scan time (or #{\mathcal{O}(N D + N \log K)} with bounded top-#{K} selection) on #{\mathbb{R}^D}.
}

\p{
  Local-scale dissimilarities replace stationary lengthscales with conformal radius normalization (Zelnik-Manor & Perona [[ZP04]](https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2004/hash/40173ea48d9567f1f393b20c855bb40b-Abstract.html)), Poincaré half-space chordal invariants (Cannon et al. [[CFK+97]](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book31/files/cannon.pdf), Nickel & Kiela [[NK17]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.08039), Kisfaludi-Bak & van Wordragen [[KvW24]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.01356)), and a hybrid conformal–transport cost motivated by optimal transport (Villani [[Vil09]](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-71050-9), Peyré & Cuturi [[PC19]](https://doi.org/10.1561/2200000073)).
}

\p{
  Product spaces #{\mathcal{M} = \prod_\ell \mathcal{M}_\ell} map to solver coordinates via section–retraction charts #{\psi_\ell : \mathcal{M}_\ell \to \mathcal{U}_\ell \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{d_\ell}} and #{\rho_\ell : \mathcal{U}_\ell \to \mathcal{M}_\ell} covering positive scales (Čencov [[Cen82]](https://doi.org/10.1090/mmono/053)), circular phases, probability simplices in Bhattacharyya coordinates [[Bha43]](https://www.jstor.org/stable/25047882), and discrete codebooks via one-hot or Rademacher embeddings (Johnson & Lindenstrauss [[JL84]](https://doi.org/10.1090/conm/026/737400), Achlioptas [[Ach03]](https://doi.org/10.1016/S0022-0000(03)00025-4)). Multi-output codomains support polar Stiefel projection (Higham [[Hig86]](https://doi.org/10.1137/0907079)), orthogonal subspace shrinkage (Stein [[Ste56]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Third-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Inadmissibility-of-the-Usual-Estimator-for-the-Mean-of-a/bsmsp/1200501656), James & Stein [[JS61]](https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/berkeley-symposium-on-mathematical-statistics-and-probability/Proceedings-of-the-Fourth-Berkeley-Symposium-on-Mathematical-Statistics-and/chapter/Estimation-with-Quadratic-Loss/bsmsp/1200512173)), and split-conformal residual scaling (Papadopoulos et al. [[Pap+02]](https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-36755-1_29), Vovk et al. [[VGS05]](https://doi.org/10.1007/b106715), Lei et al. [[LGR+18]](https://doi.org/10.1080/01621459.2017.1307116)).
}

\p{
  Trust-region search (Eriksson et al. [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739)) adapts bounding side lengths from consecutive improvement and failure counters, perturbs axis-aligned coordinate subsets (RAASP [[EPG+19]](https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739), Rashidi et al. [[RJG24]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v238/rashidi24a.html)), and coordinates multi-region portfolios (Daulton et al. [[DEB+22]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v180/daulton22a.html), [[DBB20]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.05078)) through epoch-guarded ledgers and augmented Chebyshev scalarizations (Knowles [[Kno06]](https://doi.org/10.1109/TEVC.2005.851274), Paria et al. [[PKP20]](https://proceedings.mlr.press/v115/paria20a.html)).
}

\p{
  The runtime represents optimizer state as an immutable snapshot paired with a splittable 128-bit PRNG key (Claessen & Pałka [[CP13]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2503778.2503784), Steele et al. [[SLF14]](https://doi.org/10.1145/2660193.2660195)), folding rollouts via \code{scan} and executing distance and candidate kernels on portable SIMD CPU, Metal, and CUDA backends.
}
